    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-brave-hypatia-r820sf/folio-assistant.jsonld#directory/tools",
      "@type": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/bootstrap/ns#Directory",
      "name": "tools",
      "path": "tools/",
      "holdsGraph": [
        "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/ns#graphKind/tools"
      ],
      "description": "Tool definitions, one subdirectory per contributing instance (tools/<stub>/) so a composed instance adds its own without colliding on a filename. tools/index.ts merges them and is the only path a consumer imports. Authored as .ts calling defineTool so a malformed node fails at tsc; the JSON-LD and JSON Schema renderings are generated.",
      "alternateOf": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/folio-assistant.jsonld#directory/tools"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-brave-hypatia-r820sf/folio-assistant.jsonld#directory/schemas",
      "@type": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/bootstrap/ns#Directory",
      "name": "schemas",
      "path": "schemas/",
      "holdsGraph": [
        "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/ns#graphKind/schemas",
        "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/ns#graphKind/cat-harness"
      ],
      "description": "The content-object model and the per-skill JSON Schemas. Declares BOTH graphs: the schema definitions themselves, and `kg` because a schema IS a knowledge-graph node — the .ts files are part of the KG, not a separate island beside it.",
      "alternateOf": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/folio-assistant.jsonld#directory/schemas"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-brave-hypatia-r820sf/folio-assistant.jsonld#directory/bootstrap",
      "@type": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/bootstrap/ns#Directory",
      "name": "bootstrap",
      "path": "bootstrap/skills/",
      "holdsGraph": [
        "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/ns#graphKind/cat-harness"
      ],
      "description": "The bootstrap graph's SKILLS — what an agent reads before it knows whether this repository is an instance, what kind, or what for. A SECOND `cat-harness` directory beside `skills/`, and the first real use of the topical layout the declaration was built for: `kgRoots()` returns two, and every consumer reading the declaration rather than the literal `skills/` finds both with no code change. IT POINTS AT `bootstrap/skills/`, NOT `bootstrap/`, and the one level matters. `isSkillMd` is declaration-over-location: a markdown file that does not declare `$schema:` IS a skill. `bootstrap/README.md` and `bootstrap/AGENTS.md` declare neither, and they are the bootstrap instance's two DECLARED ASSETS — so a graph rooted at `bootstrap/` counts them as skill nodes and the scan contradicts a declaration that has already answered the question. Measured on this branch before the fix: `skill/AGENTS` was in the export. #422 found this from the other side and declared `skills/` inside `bootstrap/harness.json`; this entry is the same fact at the root, and it is stated twice BY DECISION (2026-09-20) rather than by necessity. THE EARLIER NOTE HERE WAS WRONG and the correction is the useful part: it said \"this instance depends on bootstrap\" is not expressible because `HarnessConfigDependenciesSchema` knows only `folioAssistant`. That key does not mean the folio-assistant PACKAGE — its own doc comment reads \"Other folio-assistant instances this folio depends on\", and bootstrap is such an instance by every test the code applies. MEASURED, not argued: writing `cat-harness/harness.config.json` with `{name: \"bootstrap\", path: \"../bootstrap\", provides: [\"skills\"]}` serves bootstrap through `LOCAL_PACKAGES` with `folio-assistant` unmoved. The zod schema needs no change at all. WHAT ACTUALLY BLOCKS IT is one directory entry, and it is not in the dependency schema: bootstrap declares `workflows/` with no `scope`, so cat-harness INHERITS it, and `materialiseDirectories` resolves an inherited path against the INHERITING instance by design — it wants `cat-harness/workflows/`, which does not exist, and `harness:dirs:check` reports `21 declared, 1 missing` and exits 1. The gap it exposes is general and this entry is only where it surfaced: a `ContentDirectory` cannot say whether it is a LAYOUT dependents should reproduce (`uploads/`, `library/`, `voices/`) or merely WHERE ITS OWN CONTENT LIVES (`workflows/`, `schemas/`, `tools/`). Simulated on a fresh folio depending on this instance, `init-folio` would create five directories it has no use for, each with a committed keep-marker — the `dh4f` shape, shipped downstream. Adding that distinction was considered and DECLINED for now: it is a schema change whose per-directory classification is a judgement about every directory here, not a bootstrap workaround. So THIS entry stays, and the thing to fix before deleting it is the convention/content distinction — not the dependency schema. The earlier note here said `resolveSkillDirs` had no caller: it has one — `discoverLocalPackages` — and the reachability it was waiting on arrived with the `scope` fix in `ownDirectories`, which until then resolved this path against the INSTANCE and so dropped it for not existing. REPOSITORY-scoped: it sits at the top of the checkout, not inside this instance, and it is deliberately never overlaid by a dependency.",
      "alternateOf": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/folio-assistant.jsonld#directory/bootstrap"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-brave-hypatia-r820sf/folio-assistant.jsonld#directory/cat-harness",
      "@type": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/bootstrap/ns#Directory",
      "name": "cat-harness",
      "path": "skills/",
      "holdsGraph": [
        "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/ns#graphKind/cat-harness"
      ],
      "description": "The knowledge graph. Skill packages, and the four other node kinds that point at them: `roles/roles.json` (a role IS a BPMN swimlane, and carries the skills its lane's activities need), `workflows/*.bpmn` + `workflows/decisions/*.dmn` (the processes those roles act in, reached through the skill that describes them), `requirements/*.json` (conformance obligations whose `satisfiedBy` names the skill or capability discharging them), `permissions/permissions.json` (what an actor may DO in any lane, as opposed to what its lane's role knows), Its audit verdicts are NOT here: they moved to the `qa` tree on 2026-09-19, because a verdict is a QA reviewer's output and placement follows provenance. Note the id is `kg` while the path is `skills/` — ids are stable across a relocation, paths are not.",
      "alternateOf": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/folio-assistant.jsonld#directory/cat-harness"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-brave-hypatia-r820sf/folio-assistant.jsonld#directory/methodology-raci",
      "@type": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/bootstrap/ns#Directory",
      "name": "methodology-raci",
      "path": "methodologies/raci/",
      "holdsGraph": [
        "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/ns#graphKind/cat-harness"
      ],
      "description": "RACI as its own subgraph beside CRDM. Three declared edge kinds — accountable, consulted, informed — over the BPMN activities and `roles.json` entries that already exist, NOT a new party registry: a second table of names would be a second answer to `who is the reviewer`, free to disagree with the registry, which is the drift the retired skill `roles:` field cost 260 dangling values (bean qif9). R is deliberately absent because a lane already declares it. Owner, 2026-09-20, bean 7o7i: exactly one Accountable per activity, ENFORCED by `check:raci`.",
      "alternateOf": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/folio-assistant.jsonld#directory/methodology-raci"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-brave-hypatia-r820sf/folio-assistant.jsonld#directory/methodology-crdm",
      "@type": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/bootstrap/ns#Directory",
      "name": "methodology-crdm",
      "path": "methodologies/crdm/",
      "holdsGraph": [
        "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/ns#graphKind/cat-harness"
      ],
      "description": "CRDM as its OWN topical subgraph — its skills and its eight diagrams together, rather than three skills in folio-core and eight .bpmn in skills/workflows/ with nothing but a filename prefix relating them. Owner, 2026-09-20: CRDM, RACI, SDLC and MADR all live under `methodologies/`, each its own subgraph. I proposed a separate `workflow-methodologies/` on the reading that PROCESS methods (how a process runs) and JUDGEMENT methods (how to decide — Kepner-Tregoe, MADR, DMN) are different axes; the owner settled it as ONE tree, which is the better answer for the reason this repository keeps relearning: two directories whose boundary needs a paragraph to explain are two answers to `where do methodologies live`, and the next author picks the wrong one. This is the layout `skillMdDirs` was built for: its own comment names `crdm` as the example of a topical directory that should cost a declaration line and no code change, and `bootstrap/skills/` was the first such root. Each methodology is declared SEPARATELY rather than declaring the parent, because `workflowDirs` resolves `<kgdir>/workflows` — declaring only the parent would leave every diagram undiscovered. SDLC and RACI are deliberately NOT declared until they have content: a declared-but-absent directory is the `dh4f` defect.",
      "alternateOf": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/folio-assistant.jsonld#directory/methodology-crdm"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-brave-hypatia-r820sf/folio-assistant.jsonld#directory/methodologies",
      "@type": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/bootstrap/ns#Directory",
      "name": "methodologies",
      "path": "methodologies/",
      "holdsGraph": [
        "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/ns#graphKind/methodology"
      ],
      "description": "Judgement methodologies the harness owns, one sub-graph each: Kepner-Tregoe for a decision, MADR for its record, DMN for the computable case. PARALLEL rather than composable — which applies is contextual, and blending them produces a house method that cites nobody. Its own graph kind rather than skills, for three properties a skill does not have: extractable (somebody else's work, adopted, so it lifts out with its declaration when the field moves on), referenced rather than inlined (two skills quoting one method is two copies free to drift), and exempt from skill criteria (skill-is-brief caps an INSTRUCTION near 280 lines; a methodology is a faithful rendering of an external standard, and truncating it to fit a house limit would misrepresent the standard). GRADE is deliberately NOT here — see smart-kg-methodologies.",
      "alternateOf": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/folio-assistant.jsonld#directory/methodologies"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-brave-hypatia-r820sf/folio-assistant.jsonld#directory/qa",
